00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:09.700
That correspondent bank goes to
another correspondent bank in Europe.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.540
That correspondent bank might go
to another correspondent bank in

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.410
another country and then eventually
it's gonna show up at the destination.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.700
There is a whole sort of back
end system behind that and

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.070
everybody has agreed though that
if you send a SWIFT message

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:26.900
then everybody is gonna
honor that message.

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.340
Okay.
So that's a way you could think about this

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.700
sort of more advance ledger technology

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.680
as something that can be used
as a replacement for SWIFT.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:37.860
Cuz SWIFT only works in certain countries
and not everybody has agreed to follow it.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.580
However, if you were sending
a digital asset like a Bitcoin,

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:45.250
you wouldn't need the whole
correspondent banking system.

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:47.090
You could just immediately
send the money and

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:51.810
that helps understand what the kind
of benefit is of having these

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:54.900
digital currencies rather
than physical currencies.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:59.900
The fiat currencies from national
countries require this back end movement

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:05.160
of money in a way that is not necessary
if you're just moving a digital currency.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:09.340
So I was very excited about this idea and
then I was going around giving these talks

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:13.530
with these very slides about a year and
a half ago.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:18.065
And then I learned that Stanford GSB
