00:13:18.065 --> 00:13:24.730
co-founded a company called Ripple,
that implemented a ledger just like this.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:28.405
That ledger allowed you to keep
not just its own native currency,

00:13:28.405 --> 00:13:33.310
which it has called the XRP, but also, any
other currency you wanted on the ledger.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:37.200
And it allowed you to use it
exactly as I've described.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:37.990
And so, today,

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:41.440
a number of financial institutions
have done just what I described.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:42.990
They've plugged into the ripple ledger.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.620
And they've allowed
people to put US dollar

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:48.020
bank balances onto the ripple ledger.

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:50.530
And then you can use them to send

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:54.120
money to anybody else in
the world in any other currency.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.480
And so then I joined as an adviser for
Ripple Labs and

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.250
now I'm on the board of directors.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:02.670
So it's a technology that I'm
really quite excited about.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.810
As an economist, the reason this
was appealing to me is that

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.600
I don't want to have a bunch of
consumers in a whole bunch of

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:14.260
stable developing countries holding
something other than their local currency.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:18.650
I would like them to think about
the financial technology innovation here

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.180
as one of being able to
move money quickly but

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.990
not of one where you're just
holding some other currency.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.960
On the other hand, if you're sitting in
Argentina or Venezuela or one of these

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:32.440
very high inflation countries, it's
a completely different kettle of fish.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.300
If you're a consumer in
one of those countries,

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:38.190
you have very limited ability to save.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:43.480
How do you save if you're a poor
consumer in a developing country,

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.210
with high inflation?

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.060
You could buy a gold necklace.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.990
That's a popular way.

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.478
Guys go to Miami and get a suitcase
full of hundred dollar bills and

00:14:51.478 --> 00:14:52.890
come around and sell them to people.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.960
And then you buy them and
stuff them under your mattress.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:58.700
That presents some security risks for
you and your family.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:04.210
If you're wealthy enough, maybe you buy
a physical asset like a car, or a tractor,

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.020
or a horse, but
if you don't have enough money to do that,

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.960
then any cash you have just gets
inflated away very quickly.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.930
Because you have very little
incentive to save, and

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:14.480
very little ability to get out of poverty.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.710
A technology innovation like this can
allow people all over the world, even

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:24.060
who don't have bank accounts, immediate
access to international financial markets.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.440
Because all they need is sort of a mobile
phone, and instead of someone walking

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:32.300
around with a suitcase full of $100 bills,
they can walk around with a phone and

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:36.420
a little bit of cash, and
exchange the cash for this digital asset.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.837
Which could then be converted into
dollars or euros, or whatever else,

00:15:39.837 --> 00:15:42.649
rather than stuffing $100
bills under your mattress.

00:15:42.649 --> 00:15:46.493
And, so this is one of the areas that's
actually happening now, and it's becoming

00:15:46.493 --> 00:15:50.461
very popular in these countries where the
existing financial systems are quite poor.

00:15:50.461 --> 00:15:54.687
This type of technology enables
a connectivity of international financial

00:15:54.687 --> 00:15:58.460
markets in a way that's just
really never been possible.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:00.120
It's also gonna make it very difficult for

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.850
those countries to
maintain capital controls.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.470
So right now, of course, recognizing this,
they don't let people leave.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:08.040
That's why you have to smuggle
the suit case full of $100 bills and

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.130
pay a bribe at the airport.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.700
So this is going to really
change the economics,

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:15.280
the macroeconomics of
those types of countries,

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:20.720
as it becomes impossible to really
enforce capital controls in the area of

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.230
digital assets that can be moved without
needing to access the banking system.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:31.740
So, let me give an example of this type
of international movement of money.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:34.520
I mean, how it would work today, and I'll
give it from the perspective of a U.S.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:38.460
consumer, even though in some
sense we have less need for

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.240
it than many other countries in the world.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:45.340
So suppose I want to
send some money to Japan.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:47.800
If it's a small dollar amount,

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:50.660
I could actually be pretty much
priced out of that transaction.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:54.350
Western Union, or something like that
is gonna charge me 20 to 30 bucks,

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:59.350
if I do a bank to bank account transfer,
that's gonna cost me 50 to 100 bucks, and

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:00.720
take a couple of days.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:06.040
So, there aren't actually great options
for making small person to person

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:10.880
international payments, and this is one
used case that people have started doing.

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:14.728
So if I wanted to send money to Japan,
what I could do is initiate

00:17:14.728 --> 00:17:19.323
a dollar transfer through a linked
account to a Bitcoin service provider.

00:17:19.323 --> 00:17:23.787
In the US, two of the very consumer
friendly ones are Circle and Coinbase and

00:17:23.787 --> 00:17:27.832
so say for example, if you want to
establish an account in Coinbase,

00:17:27.832 --> 00:17:29.749
I would link up my bank account.

00:17:29.749 --> 00:17:34.481
So I've done this myself my with Bank of
America account with Coinbase, and they

00:17:34.481 --> 00:17:38.727
sent me 73 cents in an ACH transaction and
I confirm the dollar amount so

00:17:38.727 --> 00:17:41.741
we know that we are linked up and
our ACH is working.

00:17:41.741 --> 00:17:45.398
And I send them my passport and they check
out who I am and I give them my credit

00:17:45.398 --> 00:17:49.450
card number if I want them to actually
make funds available immediately.

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:54.127
So if I have an immediate need I've
got to get some money to Japan,

00:17:54.127 --> 00:17:56.520
what I can do is I can just log onto that.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:59.210
Quickly get on my account and
say oh, Coinbase.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:03.610
I want a certain sum of money, so
I want a hundred bucks, give it to me.

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:05.120
And because they've already verified me,

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.820
they'll immediately make that available
in my little online web account.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:10.190
Then I can look for

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:15.260
a quote for what is the exchange price
between dollars and Bitcoins right now?

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:16.480
So there's a few exchanges.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:18.620
Well there's actually a large
number of exchanges, but

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.120
there's a few very big ones.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.550
And one of them is called Bitstamp.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:27.110
On say the bit stamp exchange you
can look out and see the order book.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:30.210
Some people want to buy bitcoins,
other people want to sell bitcoins.

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:34.710
And so you can see how many
bitcoins would I get for my dollar.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:36.560
And I can place an order and

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:41.180
then within a few minutes I can
exchange my dollars for bitcoins.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:44.690
And in the case of Coinbase they'll
help me do that transaction.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.697
So a few minutes later then the bitcoins
show up in my Coinbase account.

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:55.720
Now, all I need is the bitcoin
address of my friend in Japan.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:19:00.240
I tell Coinbase,
send my bitcoins there, and boom,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.940
those bitcoins are gone,
my friend in Japan has the thing of value.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.600
At that point,
we can walk away, we're done.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.220
They have the thing of value.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:11.940
Now my friend in Japan, if they
want to get out of BitCoin quickly,

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:16.220
then immediately take those
BitCoins onto an exchange that

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:20.940
operates in the BitCoin
to Yen currency pair.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:23.310
On that exchange, that would make
another currency exchange and

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.410
get their yen out and go home, okay.

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:29.960
So in a very short period of time,
we would have moved the thing of value.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.140
There could be delays getting in and
getting out.

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.100
Like if I didn't have my
coin based account set up or

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.410
if there wasn't something
like that in Japan,

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:38.120
they might have to wait a little bit
to turn the bitcoin to dollars, but

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.200
the point is, very,
very quickly they have an asset.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.710
There's a few things you have
to worry about along the way.

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:46.410
Like you don't want the exchange to
go bust while you're in the middle of

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:46.970
a transaction.

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:49.180
That would be bad,
you would lose your money.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:53.550
But you're not gonna be on that exchange
very long at least unless you want to be.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:55.700
So you really have to
trust these exchanges for

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.890
a relatively short period of time.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:00.200
You might also worry about
fluctuations in the currency.

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.370
People say, well bitcoin used to be
a thousand dollars a bitcoin and now it's

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:07.070
300 dollars a bitcoin, doesn't that make
it really bad for sending money to Japan?

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:09.780
I can say well no, actually,

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:13.390
because it really doesn't change very
much in the course of ten minutes.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.060
And that's really all we needed to know.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.970
We needed to know is it gonna be stable
while we process this transaction?

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:20.735
While we get in and out of BitCoin?

00:20:20.735 --> 00:20:23.757
And so this is my,
from an economics perspective,

00:20:23.757 --> 00:20:26.280
I think of this as a payment rail.

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:29.800
This is a movement,
a way to move money, and

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:35.500
I got it from here to Japan instantly,
and that was very powerful.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.690
But I didn't have to hold it.

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:42.560
It's not something that I need to hold or
use as money in my daily life for

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.070
it to be useful.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.780
It can be used for that, too,
but it doesn't have to be.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:51.560
So this is one really crucial use case.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:55.260
Another use case is of course just that
you and I could use it with each other to

00:20:55.260 --> 00:21:00.640
split a check, split a taxi,
to move money between each other.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:04.010
Now that all of us are starting to ditch
our wallets and only carry our phones,

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:05.189
that would be very convenient.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:07.920
In the US there are some other options.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.790
Like Venmo allows you to do that.

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:12.690
In Australia and in Europe, actually,

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:14.660
just bank to bank transfers
are instant and free.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:15.850
Why don't we have that?

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:17.730
Good question.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:23.290
That's a question about competition and
market power but we'll get back to that.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:27.410
So in principle you have other ways to
do that but it's one nice way to do it.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:30.940
So I'll mention right now I'm in the
process of running an experiment with MIT

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:34.738
undergraduates where we've given 100
dollars worth of bitcoins to a majority of

00:21:34.738 --> 00:21:36.126
MIT undergraduates.

00:21:36.126 --> 00:21:38.790
And so we're gonna watch over time and
see if when

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:42.230
all of their friends have them if they
find this a very useful thing to do.

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:44.776
But it turns out a lot of them,
a majority of them,

00:21:44.776 --> 00:21:49.954
were already using Venmo which is
this peer to peer cash app and

00:21:49.954 --> 00:21:54.450
so they already had some pretty good
cashless ways to split checks as well.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:58.400
So, it'll be interesting to see
whether BitCoin they find superior or

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.595
inferior to the mechanisms
that they already have.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:05.640
So let me say a few more words
about uses cases of this.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:11.150
So as I mentioned this international
transaction is a really big use case.

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:14.070
So this table just shows
sort of the bank fees.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:15.440
There's outgoing and

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.140
incoming wire transfer fees if you
wanna move money internationally.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:24.050
So it's not just the un-banked that have
to pay Western Union a lot of money.

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:27.710
In fact, Western Union is cheaper
than what your bank will offer you.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:31.210
Remittances, of course, is a huge market.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:34.911
So the formal remittance market is roughly
six or seven hundred billion dollars.

00:22:34.911 --> 00:22:38.825
The informal remittance market
is north of a trillion.

00:22:38.825 --> 00:22:39.456
And again,

00:22:39.456 --> 00:22:43.368
these fees are being paid by some of
the world's most vulnerable people,

00:22:43.368 --> 00:22:48.050
people who are going off to work 70 or
80 hours a week and remit money home.

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:49.400
They might have emergencies.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:52.720
Maybe the folks on both ends are not
that organized with budgeting.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:57.530
And they may have unforeseen needs where
they don't have any kind of buffer, so

00:22:57.530 --> 00:23:01.480
it's fairly frequent that people
are having to send these remittances and

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:04.040
pay fees maybe more often
than you would like.

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:07.930
And they've become
a relatively onerous burden.

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:09.030
Why are they expensive?

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.300
Well on the back end,

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:17.740
it actually does take a fair bit of time
to move money from one country to another.

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:21.780
So Western Union and other types of
remittance companies will generally make

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:24.410
the money available to
the consumer on the other end but

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:27.170
it actually takes them a couple
of days to get their money.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:31.090
That means they have to hold capital and
they have to hedge their exchange rate

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:35.670
risk during that time period,
which adds to the cost of the transaction.

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:39.003
If you were able to
eliminate those time delays,

00:23:39.003 --> 00:23:43.002
you would reduce the cost of
operating these businesses.

00:23:43.002 --> 00:23:46.084
So some of the businesses
that are being proposed and

00:23:46.084 --> 00:23:50.567
built right now are crypto-currency
based solutions Where you might build

00:23:50.567 --> 00:23:54.138
a network of on ramps that change
local currency to Bitcoin,

00:23:54.138 --> 00:23:57.940
move it internationally using Bitcoin and
then cash out again.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:02.390
So you can imagine a set of say
newsstands in one country and

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:08.170
a set of florists in another country that
say hey let's start a remittance network.

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:10.869
Maybe my brother moved to some other
country and he's got the florists and

00:24:10.869 --> 00:24:11.880
I've got the newsstands.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:15.210
And so, we're gonna get together and
we're gonna send the money.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:18.970
We don't have any capital though,
we're just kinda scraping by in our little

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:21.670
businesses, but
we do have a lot of retail outlets.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:25.530
So, without any capital I can actually
operate a remittance business

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:30.000
because I can move the money instantly and
I don't bear any risk from doing that.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.410
So, I don't actually have to
have a lot of capital nor

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:35.550
do I have to have,
my agents hold a lot of capital.

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:40.276
And so we are seeing kinda informal
entrepreneurial activity happening,

00:24:40.276 --> 00:24:44.788
which can make a lot of countries
better connected than they are today.

00:24:44.788 --> 00:24:47.689
People are also using Ripple for this.

00:24:47.689 --> 00:24:52.811
And so, there's a couple of remittance
companies now that are looking into and

00:24:52.811 --> 00:24:55.723
prototyping, using Ripple as a back-end.

00:24:55.723 --> 00:25:00.298
Where they don't even have to actually
touch any virtual currency, but

00:25:00.298 --> 00:25:05.330
they can just use Ripple as a payment
network to move money instantly overseas.

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:08.870
So, how does this affect society?

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:10.620
Well, there's lots of applications.

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:13.840
So, we've talked about back-end
money movements like remittances,

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:15.240
also multinational firms.

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:19.120
So, even very large firms end up
paying tens or even hundreds of

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:22.960
millions of dollars in fees as they move
money back and forth around the world.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.180
Every time they move a little money,
they have to pay to pay a fee.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:30.810
If you have a trillion dollars of
transactions moving back and forth across

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:34.980
your balance sheet over the year, even a
small percentage of that adds up to a lot.

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:39.514
And so, corporate treasurers of major
companies are very unhappy with the fees

00:25:39.514 --> 00:25:40.808
that they have to pay.

00:25:40.808 --> 00:25:42.930
Cuz they say, what service are you
providing me with anyways?

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:46.343
You're just moving my money from me to me,
but

00:25:46.343 --> 00:25:52.128
why am I getting taxed these large sums
of money for this very minimal service.

00:25:52.128 --> 00:25:56.302
And so they also use these very complex
IT systems to keep track of their

00:25:56.302 --> 00:25:57.520
money internally.

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:01.080
Recently Citibank lost $300
million dollars in Mexico.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.795
You lose things with
these complex IT systems.

00:26:04.795 --> 00:26:08.275
And if everything was on sorta a ledger,
say, an internal ledger for

00:26:08.275 --> 00:26:11.095
the company that was
public within the company.

00:26:11.095 --> 00:26:13.355
And everybody could see where
the assets were moving.

00:26:13.355 --> 00:26:14.665
You wouldn't lose the money.

00:26:14.665 --> 00:26:19.655
And you also wouldn't have to pay
the fees or pay the IT support costs.

00:26:19.655 --> 00:26:22.525
So there's lots of ideas being
explored in this direction,

00:26:22.525 --> 00:26:25.695
from keeping track of development
funds and government funds.

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:35.468
To collections of firms that are all
trying to keep their books in a novel way.

00:26:35.468 --> 00:26:37.980
Finally, there's this idea
of sorta programmable money.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:40.745
So getting back to the very
beginning of the talk,

00:26:40.745 --> 00:26:44.245
we said what was really novel and
different about Bitcoin or

00:26:44.245 --> 00:26:49.465
any other kinda purely digital
asset that was kept on a ledger.

00:26:49.465 --> 00:26:53.185
And there are many besides Bitcoin and
Ripples Currency XRP.

00:26:53.185 --> 00:26:57.315
What was special about those things is
that you can actually operate on them

00:26:57.315 --> 00:26:59.215
as an individual without middlemen.

00:26:59.215 --> 00:27:01.305
And you might think, well,
I don't mind the middleman.

00:27:01.305 --> 00:27:02.385
I kinda trust my banks.

00:27:02.385 --> 00:27:04.180
I'm not that worried about it.

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.750
But actually the middlemen
slow things down, as well.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:12.160
And it's impossible to write a computer
program that's going to execute

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:15.580
a complicated financial transaction
between lots of different financial

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:16.590
institutions.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.360
They each have their own systems and
their own delays.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.570
But once the money is all digital,

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:24.580
you can actually write computer
programs on top of it.

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:27.940
To automatically execute transactions,

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:30.550
and that's gonna allow
very new things to happen.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:34.830
Just a very simple example is multi-sig,
so I want two people to sign a check.

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:38.760
So I can write a computer program that
says here's this money in this Bitcoin

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:44.200
account, but it can only be moved out if
two people both put in their passwords.

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:47.761
That could help protect you from
being mugged or anything else for

00:27:47.761 --> 00:27:48.998
your digital assets.

00:27:48.998 --> 00:27:51.962
Because somebody else would have to put
in their password too in order to get

00:27:51.962 --> 00:27:53.760
the money out,
it could also protect firms.

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:59.835
One of the things that's getting built
right now are actual platforms for

00:27:59.835 --> 00:28:04.322
programmable money so
Ripple Labs has a platform called Codius.

00:28:04.322 --> 00:28:06.530
There's another one called
Ethereum out there.

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:10.560
Both of them are trying to help people
write what they call smart contracts,

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:13.820
or computer programs
that distribute money.

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:17.550
The Codius platform would allow
you to write a program, and

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:20.640
it will make it easy for
people to verify the program works.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:24.640
They would say, if these five things
happen, then this person gets paid.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:29.770
If these other three things happen,
this other person gets paid.

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:30.660
So we could just, for

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:34.220
example, you could think about some
sorta complex financial derivative.

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:39.280
We could take as an input
the Bloomberg feed of stock prices.

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:42.890
And we could make very complex bets
about well, if this stock goes up and

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.530
this stock goes down, and
this other thing happens, and

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:50.150
the national weather service says its
sunny today, then this person gets paid.

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:53.270
And if these other things
happen some one else gets paid.

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:55.220
So that's going to really
reduce the barriers for

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:58.410
entry for
creating financial derivative products.

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:01.860
And it's going to allow more
people to hedge more complicated

00:29:01.860 --> 00:29:05.300
situations where theres not enough
people right now to make the market

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:08.120
worth while to create the asset and
have it verified.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.570
There are lots of other
applications right now.

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:14.540
There's mini startups with some of
the smartest people in the world trying to

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:18.500
figure out clever things to do
once money becomes programmable.

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:24.160
And that's really where we all have to use
our imaginations for, what could I do?

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:28.600
If hundreds of people who didn't know each
other all over the world could participate

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:31.680
in a state contingent
financial transaction.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:33.720
That paid different people
in different circumstances.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.170
It just wasn't possible before.

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:37.955
So we're really not sure what
people are going to do with it.

00:29:37.955 --> 00:29:39.435
So what is that going to do?

00:29:39.435 --> 00:29:42.715
Well we're going to see various
types of changes in industry.

00:29:42.715 --> 00:29:45.925
We might see diverticalization
of international transfers, so

00:29:45.925 --> 00:29:48.755
again the flower stand and
the news stand can get together.

00:29:48.755 --> 00:29:51.885
We don't need to have big
financial institutions do this.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:55.670
Banks also may lose
some of their business,

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.590
because we can use,
we can hold money in Bitcoin.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:00.690
And we don't need to hold
it in our checking account.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:03.460
And if we don't need to,
if we trust the computer algorithms, and

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:05.620
we don't need to trust an institution.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:08.860
Then I may be willing to hold
my money lots of other places,

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:13.480
besides the zero interest checking
account, that I have at my bank.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:14.430
And as banks start,

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:17.890
their services start to get unbundled
that's been a frequent phenomenon with

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:21.660
the internet is something that used to
all go together now gets broken apart.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.930
Their business models may change.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.650
And finally we are going to see
just lots of new services so

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:30.040
you know things like public
recording of messages.

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:34.479
I can pick an entry on the blockchain
that verifies some fact or some piece of

00:30:34.479 --> 00:30:38.855
information in the secured public
ledger forever, for everyone to see.

00:30:38.855 --> 00:30:41.070
And we can write contracts based on that.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:43.370
We can provide low-friction
escrow services.

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:46.610
When you buy a house, there's a title
agent, you pay them a bunch of money, and

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:47.780
they hold on to your money and

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:51.760
they only give the money to the seller
when the title changes over to the buyer.

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:57.070
We only do that for expensive stuff, but
we could do it for much cheaper stuff.

00:30:57.070 --> 00:31:00.020
So air B &amp; B expanded partly because they,

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:03.740
unlike previous sites that did the same
service, they kept the money in escrow.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:07.290
And the person only get paid once you
actually had your stay in the house.

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:09.190
They escrowed the funds.

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:12.390
So you can imagine lots
of other transactions,

00:31:12.390 --> 00:31:15.930
smaller value transactions where
I'm not sure I trust you enough.

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:20.010
And I'm not gonna just wire money to
somebody in China to give Mandarin lessons

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:22.820
before I go on my business trip,
because I might wire it and

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:24.150
never see the person again.

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:26.310
But we can put it in escrow and

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:30.020
make that transaction possible in a way
that wouldn't have been possible before or

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:32.730
would have required a platform
to mediate and then take a cut.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.760
Tracking flows of funds and

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:41.100
generally I think the theme is that we've
seen from Uber to Airbnb to TaskRabbit and

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:44.450
all the other great new
internet platforms out there.

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:49.140
Information and contractility, making
things easier and more frictionless.

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:52.940
And allowing people to transact with each
other without such a level of trust.

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:56.810
Has enabled huge markets to grow
where no market existed before.

00:31:56.810 --> 00:32:00.960
And digital money is going to allow
that to happen in new services and

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:05.310
applications as well as making
existing one more efficient.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:08.800
So this is basically why I am so
excited about this technology.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.040
Not because I think you should speculate
in Bitcoin and buy it at 300 and

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:13.620
hope it goes to a thousand.

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:16.340
But because, fundamentally,
it's like the internet.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:20.320
A new technology that allows things to
happen that couldn't happen before.

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:23.780
And we have a whole range of some of
the world's brightest entrepreneurs,

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:26.560
trying to figure out what to build on it.

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:28.600
The first wave of them may go bust.

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:31.270
But eventually,
I have a lot of faith, that.

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:35.944
The way that we move money is going
to catch up with the way that we move

00:32:35.944 --> 00:32:37.434
information today.

00:32:37.434 --> 00:32:38.700
Thanks very much.

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:43.480
[MUSIC]

